---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Feedback from '../components/Feedback.astro';
import FlowBands from '../components/FlowBands.astro';
import ProposalLimitations from '../components/ProposalLimitations.astro';
const principles=[['Personal use stays free.','Personal use and remixing would not trigger royalties. The model is intended to keep experimentation accessible.'],['Earnings trigger an obligation.','Revenue-generating uses would contribute under terms still being defined. Building something and earning from it are different moments.'],['Contributions relate to revenue.','The royalty basis, thresholds, and rates remain open design questions. The goal is to align the contribution with real earnings.'],['Distribution should be automatic.','Tributary would aim to track dependencies and route royalties to their creators. The mechanism is not yet implemented.'],['Attribution follows the code.','Creator credit should distinguish reuse, adaptation, and known references. A credit record would not, by itself, establish a royalty obligation.']];
---
<Layout title="Open Royalty License proposal — Tributary" description="Explore the draft principles of the Open Royalty License, a proposed royalty model for shared software. Includes clean-room development, attribution gaps, and other limitations. Community feedback is welcome."><section class="document-masthead license-masthead"><FlowBands id="license-flow" tone="warm"/><div class="document-header wrap"><a href="/" class="eyebrow back-link">TRIBUTARY / THE STANDARD</a><div class="document-status"><span class="status-dot"></span>Whitepaper proposal · Community feedback welcome</div><h1>Open Royalty<br />License<span class="blue-text">.</span></h1><p class="document-intro">A proposed agreement for shared software.<br />Free to build on. A contribution when you earn.</p></div></section><div class="reading-page wrap"><div class="document-grid"><aside class="document-nav" aria-label="On this page"><span class="eyebrow">IN THIS DRAFT</span><a href="#principles">The principles</a><a href="#questions">Open questions</a><a href="#limitations">Where this could break</a><a href="#feedback">Give feedback</a><div class="draft-aside">A living proposal.<br />Help us get it right.</div></aside><div class="reading-body"><div class="draft-notice">This is a statement of principles, not final license terms. Legal text is in progress.</div><p class="lead">The ORL is part of a whitepaper proposal for royalties when products built on licensed code earn revenue. We’re sharing these draft principles to seek feedback from the community; the terms are still being developed.</p><section id="principles" class="principles">{principles.map(([title, body], i) => <article><span class="principle-number">0{i+1}</span><div><h2>{title}</h2><p>{body}</p></div></article>)}</section><section id="questions" class="document-questions"><span class="eyebrow">WHAT WE STILL NEED TO SOLVE</span><h2>The hard questions are part of the work.</h2><p>How do we define revenue? How should contributions from indirect dependencies be attributed? How do we weigh a reusable button against an entire system? What would compatibility with existing licenses require? When should an AI rewrite preserve credit, and when would it owe compensation? How can attribution be audited?</p><p>We’re inviting maintainers, builders, and people with relevant experience to challenge the model before these principles become terms.</p></section><ProposalLimitations /><Feedback heading="Which assumption should we challenge?" /></div></div></div></Layout>
